WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.660
You eat a turkey sandwich, and some of it goes on to become part of you.

2
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.020
How can a wheat plant and a bird turn into part of a person?

3
00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.530
Zoom into the bread.

4
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.610
Its starch is a long chain of small sugar blocks.

5
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.270
Now zoom into the turkey.

6
00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:27.450
Its proteins are chains of a different kind of block, amino acids of many kinds.

7
00:00:28.583 --> 00:00:30.863
Each small block is called a monomer.

8
00:00:32.003 --> 00:00:33.683
A long chain of them is a polymer.

9
00:00:35.103 --> 00:00:38.423
Think of beads on a necklace, though real chains fold and coil.

10
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.207
In your gut, the chains come apart.

11
00:00:43.427 --> 00:00:46.707
A water molecule slips into one link, and the link breaks.

12
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:50.327
Splitting with water is called hydrolysis.

13
00:00:51.627 --> 00:00:53.347
One water, one broken link.

14
00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:56.467
Here is a chain of six sugar blocks.

15
00:00:57.847 --> 00:01:00.827
How many water molecules will it take to break every link?

16
00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:11.953
Five. Six blocks have only five links between them.

17
00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:14.613
Each water breaks one link.

18
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:18.473
So it is one water per link, not one per block.

19
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:22.867
The freed blocks leave the gut and reach your body's cells.

20
00:01:24.207 --> 00:01:25.667
There, the job turns around.

21
00:01:27.027 --> 00:01:30.027
Instead of taking chains apart, the cell builds new ones.

22
00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.090
Inside the cell, the blocks join into new chains.

23
00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:37.390
Compare the free blocks with the finished chain.

24
00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.530
One water's worth of atoms is missing at each link.

25
00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:46.290
Building by removing water is called dehydration synthesis.

26
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:49.433
Now count it the other way.

27
00:01:50.793 --> 00:01:52.993
Breaking a chain of six blocks took five waters.

28
00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:57.793
A chain of six built from free blocks is five waters short.

29
00:01:59.073 --> 00:02:00.453
One per link, either way.

30
00:02:01.433 --> 00:02:03.733
Joining blocks does not happen for free.

31
00:02:05.173 --> 00:02:06.173
Joining takes energy.

32
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:11.673
In cells, each kind of molecule is built by its own energy-using route.

33
00:02:12.893 --> 00:02:16.313
So the real steps usually release other products, not water.

34
00:02:17.553 --> 00:02:19.953
The water count is bookkeeping for the atoms.

35
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:22.633
Taking apart adds water.

36
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:26.193
A built chain is one water short per link.

37
00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:28.627
Look at the new protein.

38
00:02:30.007 --> 00:02:33.187
Its amino acids sit in a different order from the turkey's.

39
00:02:34.507 --> 00:02:37.227
A turkey protein does not become your protein.

40
00:02:38.587 --> 00:02:39.687
Its blocks are reused.

41
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:47.600
Cells use four kinds of block: simple sugars, amino acids, nucleotides, and the

42
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.580
fatty acids and glycerol of fats.

43
00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.100
They build four classes of large molecule.

44
00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.420
The text sorts out which block builds which.

45
00:02:57.433 --> 00:03:00.393
So the sandwich does become you, block by block.

46
00:03:01.793 --> 00:03:04.453
Digestion adds one water for each link it breaks.

47
00:03:05.933 --> 00:03:09.853
Counted from its free blocks, each new chain is one water short per link.
